<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Lishan Yang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The University of Adelaide
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nam Kha Nguygen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The University of Adelaide
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Po Hu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Central China Normal University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Wei Emma Zhang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The University of Adelaide
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yanjun Shu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Harbin Institute of Technology
<span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correspondence:</span><a href="mailto:email@domain" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">wei.e.zhang@adelaide.edu.au</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mong Yuan Sim
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The University of Adelaide
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Weitong Chen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The University of Adelaide
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Foundation models have demonstrated remarkable performance across a wide range of tasks, yet their large parameter sizes pose challenges for practical deployment, especially in decentralized environments. Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT), such as Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), reduces local computing and memory overhead, making it attractive for federated learning. However, existing federated LoRA methods typically assume uniform rank configurations and unimodal inputs, overlooking two key real-world challenges: (1) heterogeneous client resources have different LoRA ranks, and (2) multimodal data settings with potentially missing modalities.
In this work, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FediLoRA</span>, a simple yet effective framework for federated multimodal fine-tuning under heterogeneous LoRA ranks and missing modalities. FediLoRA introduces a dimension-wise aggregation strategy that reweights LoRA updates without information dilution during aggregation. It also includes a lightweight layer-wise model editing method that selectively incorporates global parameters to repair local components which improves both client and global model performances.
Experimental results on three multimodal benchmark datasets demonstrate that FediLoRA achieves superior performance over competitive baselines in both global and personalized settings, particularly in the presence of modality incompleteness.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Foundation models (FMs) have demonstrated impressive generalization across diverse downstream tasks in both unimodal and multimodal domains. However, their massive parameter size and high computational demand pose significant barriers to fine-tuning and deployment.
Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) techniques such as
adapters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Houlsby et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, prefix-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Liang (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2022)</a></cite>, and particularly Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2022a)</a></cite> have gained increasing attention for efficiently adapting large-scale models. LoRA introduces trainable low-rank matrices to approximate gradient updates while keeping the original pre-trained weights frozen. This design leverages the assumption that over-parameterized models possess a low intrinsic dimensionality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2018)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aghajanyan et al. (2021)</a></cite>. By reducing the number of trainable parameters, LoRA significantly lowers the computational overhead required for fine-tuning large foundation models thus gaining increasing adoption in real-world applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Federated Learning (FL)  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">McMahan et al. (2017a)</a></cite> offers a decentralized solution by enabling multiple clients to collaboratively train a global model while keeping data local. Multimodal Federated Learning (MFL) extends FL to clients with diverse modality combinations (e.g., image–text) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zong et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Li (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al. (2024)</a></cite>. A key challenge of MFL is handling <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">missing modalities</span>. While prior works such as modality reconstruction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. (2023)</a></cite>, contrastive learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2023)</a></cite>, and prototype exchange <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bao et al. (2024)</a></cite> have addressed missing modalities, these approaches generate significant computation overhead when applying to foundation models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The integration of LoRA and FL has gained attention, enabling efficient adaptation of large models under computation and privacy constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
Nevertheless, most existing approaches assume homogeneous LoRA configurations—i.e., all clients share the same rank for their LoRA modules. This assumption may not be realistic in real-world federated environments given varying client resources and data distributions.
Recent methods such as HetLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho et al. (2024)</a></cite>, FLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, and FlexLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2024)</a></cite> explore heterogeneous LoRA however lack effective mechanisms to handle scenarios with missing modalities in the multimodal applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the research effort of addressing missing modality in heterogenous Federated LoRA - a common scenario in the era of large foundation models - is missing. </span>
To this end, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FediLoRA</span> to fill this gap. Our method is motivated by the observation that the global model achieves comparable performance when trained on either full-modality or missing-modality settings (Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Preliminary Experiments ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). We attribute this robustness to the averaging effect of FedAvg aggregation - the de facto standard aggregation method in federated learning, which aggregates client updates using a weighted average based on local data size.
To extend this benefit to heterogeneous settings, we retain the core idea of FedAvg and adapt it to fit the heterogeneous LoRA and propose a dimension-wise reweighting mechanism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the global model’s stability, we observe that client models suffer from a notable performance gap, particularly under missing-modality scenarios. This is due to both local data sparsity and FedAvg’s emphasis on optimizing global performance. Given our success in handling missing modalities on the global model, we further explore whether aligning local models to the global model could improve both global and clients’ performance. This leads us to adopt a simple yet effective solution: repairing local LoRA layers using their global counterparts. This layer-wise model editing strategy efficiently transfers useful global knowledge to clients without introducing additional training overhead (Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Analysis for Efficiency and Resource Consumption ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a dimension-wise weighted aggregation strategy that enables effective heterogeneous federated LoRA while preserving model robustness to missing modalities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a layer-wise model editing technique that uses global LoRA parameters to correct the local components, enhancing client model performance without additional computational cost.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments on three multimodal FL benchmarks, demonstrating our proposed FediLoRA outperforms existing methods in both global and client performances under missing modality conditions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Federated LoRA</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In federated learning, a central server orchestrates the communication and aggregation processes, acting as the coordination hub for collecting and distributing model updates from clients<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Federated learning systems are broadly categorized into centralized (CFL) and decentralized (DFL) frameworks. This work adopts the more commonly used CFL setting.</span></span></span>. Initially, the server broadcasts its model parameters<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            In many FL settings, the transmitted parameters may be adapters, continuous prompts, or the parameters of lightweight prompt-generating networks.</span></span></span> to all participating clients. Each client then updates the model locally using its private data and transmits the modified parameters back to the server. The server aggregates these updates to produce a new global model, which is then redistributed to clients, completing one communication round.
FedAvg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">McMahan et al. (2017b)</a></cite> is the standard aggregation method to get global updates:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{global}^{t+1}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\frac{|\mathcal{D}_{k}|}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}|\mathcal{D}_{j}|}\cdot\mathcal{M}_{k}^{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{global}^{t+1}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\frac{|\mathcal{D}_{k}|}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}|\mathcal{D}_{j}|}\cdot\mathcal{M}_{k}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{1,\ldots,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th client and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is its local data. <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{k}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{k}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is model parameters of the <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th client at round <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{global}^{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{global}^{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the global model parameter at round <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>+1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Federated LoRA is a paradigm that combines PEFT via Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) with federated learning. Each client <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> fine-tunes a shared foundation model locally by learning client-specific low-rank matrices.
Let <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a frozen weight matrix in the foundation model. LoRA introduces a low-rank decomposition:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{k}=B_{k}A_{k},\quad A_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{k}\times n},\;B_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r_{k}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{k}=B_{k}A_{k},\quad A_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{k}\times n},\;B_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r_{k}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the local LoRA rank used by client <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The client updates only <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> using its local data <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> while keeping <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> fixed.
After local updates, each client uploads its low-rank update <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to the server. In the standard setting (homogeneous rank), the server performs weighted aggregation using FedAvg to obtain the global update <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recent works turn to transmitting <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to further reduce the communication cost, turning the update of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{K}\left(B_{1}A_{1}+B_{2}A_{2}+\cdots+B_{K}A_{K}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{K}\left(B_{1}A_{1}+B_{2}A_{2}+\cdots+B_{K}A_{K}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{K}\left(B_{1}+B_{2}+\cdots+B_{K}\right)\frac{1}{K}\left(A_{1}+A_{2}+\cdots+A_{K}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{K}\left(B_{1}+B_{2}+\cdots+B_{K}\right)\frac{1}{K}\left(A_{1}+A_{2}+\cdots+A_{K}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> - we omit the weights here for simplicity as the weighting scheme varies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The works of heterogeneous LoRA follow this efficient way, thus an alignment is required before aggregation - as <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> may vary across clients. For example, HetLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho et al. (2024)</a></cite> transmits <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and applies zero-padding to these two matrics to match the largest shared rank respectively before averaging. On the global server, the aggregated low-rank update <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> is then truncated and redistributed back to clients.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Preliminary Experiments</h3>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/motivation/moti_global_llava.png" id="S2.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span>Global performance between full and 60% missing modality training.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/motivation/moti_global_per_llava.png" id="S2.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span>Client performance comparison among different editing strategies.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>(a) shows the performance gap among global models remains relatively small under missing modality conditions. (b) The experiment investigates the impact of different LoRA editing strategies on local client performance. The blue curve represents the global performance used as a reference while there is a huge gap compared with None editing.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Averaging aggregation mitigates the effect of missing modality.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the global model and client models often exhibit significant performance gaps when using FedAvg (or its variants), due to FedAvg’s focus on global optimization, this observation motivates us to investigate whether FedAvg may help mitigate the adverse effects of missing modalities on the global server. Specifically, we hypothesize that the noise introduced by missing modalities can be diluted through the averaging nature of FedAvg aggregation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To test this hypothesis, we conduct a preliminary experiment in the Federated LoRA setting. The evaluation is carried out under a homogeneous-rank configuration to align with the standard FedAvg aggregation strategy, following the setup of FedIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Experiments are performed on the image captioning dataset SAM-LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023a)</a></cite>, considering both full-modality and missing-modality (60% missing) training scenarios.
Figure <a href="#S2.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2.2 Preliminary Experiments ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> presents the global model’s performance under this setting. We observe that while the global model initially shows instability in the missing-modality setting, it gradually recovers and approaches the full-modality performance after sufficient communication rounds.
We believe this effect is attributed to the nature of FedAvg - the averaging process inherently reinforces common patterns shared across clients while smoothing out individual client-specific noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Collins et al. (2022)</a></cite>. Consequently, even if some clients lack specific modalities or produce noisier updates, the aggregation process effectively pulls the global model back toward a stable, generalizable representation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Simple model editing is effective.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by PFedEdit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al. (2024)</a></cite><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              PFedEdit aims at personalization (i.e., focus on client performances) and its iterative method is on small deep learning models, which is computationally expensive for large foundation models. </span></span></span>, we tend to test the effectiveness of the simple model editing to preserve the model robustness.
We first identify the layer with the lowest cosine similarity between the global and local models. Based on this, we apply two simple editing strategies: <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Half-editing</span>, where the selected client layer parameters are updated as a weighted average of the last round global layer parameter of the current local layer, and <span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Full-editing</span>, where the entire local layer is replaced by the corresponding global layer.
Figure <a href="#S2.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 2.2 Preliminary Experiments ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> shows the edited client model performances under 60% missing modality training compared to the global model performance.
We computed the weighted average of each client’s local performance to obtain the client performance in this evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is evident that missing modalities significantly degrade the performance of client models and the editing could help improve the client performance.
This result demonstrates that direct model editing —specifically, replacing the degraded client parameters with their stable global counterparts can effectively compensate for modality sparsity at the client level. By selectively overriding parts of local models (e.g., LoRA adapters) with globally aggregated parameters, shared capabilities can be recovered without full retraining.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>FediLoRA</h2>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/aggregation.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/163;" width="405" height="163" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Dimension-wise re-weighting of FediLoRA. <math id="S3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}=\frac{|\mathcal{D}_{k}|}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}|\mathcal{D}_{j}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}=\frac{|\mathcal{D}_{k}|}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}|\mathcal{D}_{j}|}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the weights in FedAvg.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by these observations, we propose <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FediLoRA</span>, a federated LoRA framework that combines dimension-wise averaging aggregation with layer-wise model editing. Our approach aims to retain the inherent strength of FedAvg in mitigating the adverse effects of missing modalities and extends its applicability to heterogeneous LoRA ranks by reorganizing rank-specific dimension weights during aggregation.
In addition,
we introduce a lightweight editing strategy that selectively replaces degraded local parameters with their global counterparts at the layer level. This correction enables clients to better recover from modality sparsity without full retraining.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Dimension-wise Reweighting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To handle heterogeneous LoRA ranks, HetLoRA  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho et al. (2024)</a></cite> zero-pads each client’s LoRA matrices to match the highest rank across all clients (also the same as the global rank). However, this zero-padding can significantly dilute the contributions of clients with higher ranks during the averaging step in FedAvg-based aggregation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate information dilution in the FedAvg setting, we propose a <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dimension-wise weighted aggregation</span> which excludes zero-padded dimensions and reweights non-zero ones proportionally based on the client’s rank and data size.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following Equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Federated LoRA ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{k}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{k}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the local LoRA-A matrix of client <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The global rank is <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>=<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero-padded to size <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{g}\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{g}\times n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, for each row index <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\{1,2,\ldots,r_{g}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\{1,2,\ldots,r_{g}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a binary mask:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{mask}_{k}^{(d)}=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }d\leq r_{k}\\
0&amp;\text{if }r_{k}&lt;d\leq r_{g}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext>mask</mtext><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{mask}_{k}^{(d)}=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }d\leq r_{k}\\
0&amp;\text{if }r_{k}&lt;d\leq r_{g}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Given each client’s aggregation weight <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., based on local data size as introduced in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Federated LoRA ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), the normalized dimension-wise weight for client <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> at dimension <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{k}^{(d)}=\frac{\text{mask}_{k}^{(d)}\cdot p_{k}}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\text{mask}_{j}^{(d)}\cdot p_{j}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mtext>mask</mtext><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mtext>mask</mtext><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{k}^{(d)}=\frac{\text{mask}_{k}^{(d)}\cdot p_{k}}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\text{mask}_{j}^{(d)}\cdot p_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Using this reweighting, the aggregated global LoRA-A matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{g}\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{g}\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed row-wise as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{g}^{(d,:)}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\tilde{p}_{k}^{(d)}\cdot A_{k}^{(d,:)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g}^{(d,:)}=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\tilde{p}_{k}^{(d)}\cdot A_{k}^{(d,:)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The same strategy applies to the aggregation of the LoRA-B matrices. This method ensures that each dimension is aggregated only across the clients that contribute meaningful (non-zero) information to that dimension, while still respecting their overall weight in the federated system.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Layer-wise LoRA Editing</h3>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/lora-edit10.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/260;" width="476" height="260" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Layer-wise model editing. <math id="S3.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{g,t-1}^{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{g,t-1}^{y}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S3.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math>-th epoch, <math id="S3.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-th global LoRA A matrix. LoRA-Editing happens at each ending of local fine-tuning and before aggregation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
