</span></div>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the global and personalized performances under the missing modality setting.
As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2 Layer-wise LoRA Editing ‣ 3 FediLoRA ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, FediLoRA consistently outperforms the existing baselines HetLoRA and FLoRA across all datasets and missing ratios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">global evaluation</span>, FediLoRA achieves the highest performance across all settings. For instance, on the Recaps-118K dataset with 40% <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">missing raito (mr)</span>, FediLoRA obtains a BLEU score of 15.64 and an RSUM of 26.50, exceeding FLoRA by +0.92 BLEU and +1.93 RSUM. Under the 60% mr, it maintains performance with only a slight drop (BLEU 15.53, RSUM 25.47), remaining the best among all methods.
On the SAM-LLaVA dataset, where HetLoRA performance drops significantly under high missing ratios, FediLoRA demonstrates strong robustness. It improves over FLoRA by +1.00 BLEU and +1.19 RSUM under 40% mr, and by +0.23 BLEU and +0.47 RSUM under 60% mr. The largest absolute advantage is observed on the Next-Preference dataset, where FediLoRA surpasses FLoRA by +1.17 BLEU and +1.85 RSUM under 40% mr, and maintains a +1.72 BLEU and +1.67 RSUM lead under 60% mr.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">personalized evaluation</span> (i.e., client performance evaluation), FediLoRA remains competitive under both missing conditions. For example, on Recaps-118K with 40% mr, it achieves +1.11 BLEU and +3.89 RSUM over the best baseline. On SAM-LLaVA, it leads with up to +3.60 BLEU and +5.85 RSUM under 40% mr, and maintains a consistent advantage under 60% mr. Although FediLoRA performs best in most cases, we note that on the Next-Preference dataset with 60% mr, FLoRA achieves a slightly higher RSUM score (29.48 vs. 28.64), indicating its potential advantage in certain personalized settings. Across all datasets and conditions, FediLoRA achieves the most stable and highest overall performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Editing Different LoRA Matrices</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This experiment investigates the impact of editing different LoRA components, specifically the A matrix, B matrix, both, or none, and the results are summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The missing modality ratio is fixed at 60%.
Among all configurations, editing only the LoRA-A matrix consistently yields the best performance across all datasets, suggesting that the A matrix plays a more critical role in preserving global knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Notably, editing both A and B matrices offers no additional benefit and, in some cases, even leads to performance degradation. This is likely due to the over-editing or disruption of client-specific adaptations. Unsurprisingly, models without any editing perform the worst in most scenarios, except on the SAM-LLaVA dataset. The experiment results highlight the effectiveness of the model editing strategy of FediLoRA in mitigating degradation caused by missing modalities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Full-Parameter Editing vs FediLoRA Editing</h3>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/editing-part/recap_editing.png" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span>Recaps-118K</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/editing-part/next_editing.png" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span>Next-Preference</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/editing-part/sam_editing.png" id="S4.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((c)) </span>SAM-LLaVA</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/editing-part/sam_half_editing.png" id="S4.F4.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((d)) </span>SAM-LLaVA</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span> Comparing editing methods.
(a), (b) and (c) present the performance comparison between full-editing and FediLoRA on three datasets respectively. (d) shows the comparison of half-editing and FediLoRA.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Full-Parameter Editing vs FediLoRA Editing ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the personalized performance comparison between FediLoRA and full-layer editing under a 60% missing ratio on three datasets. In FediLoRA, let <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> denote the similarity between the local LoRA and the global LoRA. Full editing corresponds to setting <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0</annotation></semantics></math>, while half editing sets <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>. Unlike the experiments in the Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Preliminary Experiments ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, this experiment evaluates the personalized performance under heterogeneous rank configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across all three datasets, Recaps-118K (Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Full-Parameter Editing vs FediLoRA Editing ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>), Next-Preference (Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Full-Parameter Editing vs FediLoRA Editing ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>), and SAM-LLaVA (Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf3" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Full-Parameter Editing vs FediLoRA Editing ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(c)</span></a>) — FediLoRA consistently outperforms full-layer editing significantly. Notably, the gap is particularly evident in the SAM dataset, where the performance of full-layer editing stagnates while FediLoRA continues to improve steadily with each epoch. Specifically, Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.3 Full-Parameter Editing vs FediLoRA Editing ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a> witnesses a bit of large performance degradation after the 6-th epoch while using full-layer editing.
It can be observed that its personalized performance approaches that of FediLoRA to a certain extent while editing half layer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These results highlight a critical insight: increasing the proportion of edited parameters does not necessarily yield better performance. In contrast, FediLoRA’s selective editing strategy demonstrates superior effectiveness and robustness under challenging learning conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Comparative Study of Information Preservance</h3>

<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/norm/l2norm04.png" id="S4.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span>60% missing ratio</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/norm/l2norm06.png" id="S4.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span>40% missing ratio</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Comparison of the Global Adapter L2 Norm between HetLoRA and FediLoRA under 40% and 60% missing ratios.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we examine the amount of information retained in LoRA parameters during training, comparing FediLoRA with HetLoRA. Specifically, We track the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm of the aggregated global LoRA for each epoch, where the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-th round is the norm of initialized parameters. This experiment is done at SAM-LLaVA dataset.
FediLoRA and HetLoRA are initialized with the same parameters. From Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.4 Comparative Study of Information Preservance ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we see that after the first epoch of aggregation, a notable difference emerges in their <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norms: HetLoRA drops to around 10 under both missing modality ratios, while FediLoRA maintains a level above 20.
This showcases that compared to HetLoRA, FediLoRA is able to keep the information away from being diluted.
It is also mentioned by the authors of HetLoRA, that they aim to
balance high data complexity (abundant training data) with low-rank LoRA and low data complexity with high-rank LoRA. When clients with high data complexity use different ranks—such as both low-rank and high-rank configurations—important information from high-rank clients may be diluted during aggregation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>FediLoRA for Homogenous and Heterogeneous rank</h3>

<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>
Global performance of FediLoRA on both homogeneous and heterogeneous rank configurations (60% missing ratio).
</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:51pt;vertical-align:-22.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(5.9pt,-0.9pt) scale(1.03536804098332,1.03536804098332) ;">
