<td id="S4.T3.2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCEBFF;padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCEBFF;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">12.64</span></span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCEBFF;padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCEBFF;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">24.94</span></span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCEBFF;padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DCEBFF;"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.4.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11.85</span></span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.4.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">30.07</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further examine the performance of FediLoRA on the setting of homogeneous rank. Under the homogeneous setting, all clients use a fixed LoRA rank of 12, and under the heterogeneous setting, the clients adopt varying ranks from 4 to 32. Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.5 FediLoRA for Homogenous and Heterogeneous rank ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows its global performance.
Under the homogeneous configuration, FediLoRA achieves superior performance on the Recaps-118K and Next-Preference datasets, particularly showing noticeable gains in RSUM. For example, it reaches 27.16 vs. 25.47 RSUM on Recaps-118K and 31.34 vs. 30.07 on Next-Preference. However, on the SAM-LLaVA dataset, FediLoRA’s heterogeneous configuration outperforms its counterpart. With the total rank information preserved via dimension-wise averaging, heterogeneous rank allocation can potentially achieve better performance than the homogeneous counterpart.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although heterogeneous rank aggregation introduces additional noise compared to homogeneous rank methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, a high-rank LoRA can still positively contribute to the global model if it carries sufficiently rich information, and its information is preserved during aggregation.
In contrast, a homogeneous rank setting enforces a uniform capacity across all clients, which limits the ability of clients with richer training data to contribute more effectively to the global model. These factors introduce some uncertainty into the comparison between homogeneous and heterogeneous rank performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>LoRA for Federated Learning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LoRA has been increasingly adopted in federated learning (FL) due to its efficiency and parameter-reduction capabilities, which are especially beneficial when communication costs dominate  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tran et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2025)</a></cite>.
FedIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite> is one of the earliest efforts to integrate LoRA into FL by applying LoRA-based local updates combined with standard FedAvg aggregation.
FedDPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, FDLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al. (2024)</a></cite> and pFedLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al. (2023)</a></cite> employ multiple LoRA adapters for global and client models, aiming to improve personalization.
Very recently, A<sup id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>SMoE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tran et al. (2025)</a></cite> further supports client personalization by enabling resource-adaptive expert activation and aggregation based on expert usage patterns.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further reduce the communication cost, a line of work aggregates only one matrix in LoRA under FL setting.
FFA-LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2024b)</a></cite> fixes the randomly initialized non-zero A matrices and only fine-tunes the zero-initialized B matrices to halve the communication cost.
Different from FFA-LoRA, FedSA-LoRA  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2025)</a></cite> makes both A and B matrices trainable and only the A matrices are shared with the server for aggregation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, all these methods assume homogeneous LoRA ranks across clients, which limits their flexibility in heterogeneous environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Heterogeneous LoRA for FL </h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the homogeneous LoRA constraints the FL capability of capturing the full diversity of client contributions, a line of works using heterogeneous LoRA emerges.
In HetLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho et al. (2024)</a></cite>, during local training, the clients apply the rank self-pruning according to local resources. The global server aggregates the heterogeneous LoRA from clients by a simple zero-padding and sparcity-weighted aggregation. The global LoRA is then truncated to distribute to the clients.
The work in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Byun and Lee (2025)</a></cite> replaces the zero-padding in HetLoRA with a strategy that replicates rows and columns from the high-quality clients’ matrices to match the required dimensions, preserving the important information in high-priority clients and further improving the convergence speed.
FLoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a></cite> proposes a noise-free stacking-based aggregation method for heterogeneous LoRA without distributing the LoRA parameters back to the clients.
FlexLoRA  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2024)</a></cite> aggregates the full LoRA matrix <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math> from clients on the global server followed by a Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) to each client to select their singular vectors.
None of these works address multimodal or missing modalities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>FL for Missing Modality </h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CACMRN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. (2023)</a></cite> is the first FL framework to propose solutions for missing modalities.
They aim to achieve modality <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reconstruction</span> using existing multi-modal data by training multiple functionally diverse models.
Yu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2023)</a></cite> aim to address the issue of missing modalities in cross-modal retrieval. Clients, each with distinct modalities, learn the representation information of specific modalities and upload it to the server for <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">contrastive learning</span>.
Fed-Multimodal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al. (2023)</a></cite> is a multimodal benchmark built upon the FL algorithm. The work presented a range of models and datasets and proposed a method to initialize the missing modal vector as <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">zero</span>.
PmcmFL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bao et al. (2024)</a></cite> applied the common <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">prototype exchange</span> method of federated learning against Non-IID to the missing modality, and they store prototypes of all modalities for all classes from each client on the server. When encountering the missing modalities, it utilizes the corresponding class-specific prototypes to fill in the representations.
Additionally, they applied the MIX-UP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2018)</a></cite> method in data augmentation into the additional training dataset. In contrast, our approach does not impose restrictions on the types of multimodal tasks, meaning that we do not require the use of specific models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented FediLoRA, a simple yet effective framework for federated multimodal fine-tuning under heterogeneous LoRA ranks and missing modalities. By combining dimension-wise aggregation and lightweight layer-wise model editing, FediLoRA improves both global and personalized performance. Experiments across multiple datasets confirm its robustness under severe modality incompleteness. In future work, we will examine more multimodal foundation models under the same realistic setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While LoRA-based fine-tuning provides a lightweight and effective solution for multimodal models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, recent work has explored using Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures to address missing modality challenges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yun et al. (2025)</a></cite>. These methods leverage expert gating mechanisms to dynamically route available inputs, which is naturally suited for large language models (LLMs). Although they have shown promising results under standalone (centralized) training settings, their effectiveness in federated environments remains unexplored. In future work, we aim to investigate how MoE-based strategies can be adapted to federated settings, potentially improving robustness to modality incompleteness across distributed clients.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethics Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of the researchers’ knowledge, all datasets used in this study were sourced from publicly available benchmarks. The authors of the benchmark dataset also have obtained the license to distribute the dataset for non-malicious purposes intent which this research has abided by.</p>
</div>
</section>
