<li id="bib.bib47" class="ltx_bibitem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_role_refnum ltx_tag_bibitem">Zong et al. (2021)</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">
Linlin Zong, Qiujie Xie, Jiahui Zhou, Peiran Wu, Xianchao Zhang, and Bo Xu. 2021.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">Fedcmr: Federated cross-modal retrieval.

</span>
<span class="ltx_bibblock">In <em id="bib.bib47.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Proceedings of the 44th International ACM SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval, Virtual Event, Canada, July 11-15, 2021</em>, pages 1672–1676. ACM.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Editing K Layers of LoRA-A</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This experiment aims to assess how the number of edited LoRA-A layers, selected based on minimal similarity, influences the performance of FediLoRA in both global and personalized contexts.
The evaluation is conducted on SAM-LLaVA dataset with 60% missing ratio.
We evaluate four configurations of Min K (k=1, 3, 5, 7) —where K denotes the number of LoRA layers with the lowest similarity chosen for editing. Figure <a href="#A1.F6.sf1" title="In Figure 6 ‣ Appendix A Editing K Layers of LoRA-A ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(a)</span></a> illustrates the global performance across these configurations, while Figure <a href="#A1.F6.sf2" title="In Figure 6 ‣ Appendix A Editing K Layers of LoRA-A ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(b)</span></a> depicts the corresponding personalized performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/layer_num/layernum_global.png" id="A1.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span>Global</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/layer_num/layernum_per.png" id="A1.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/397;" width="476" height="397" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span>Client</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Comparison of editing more layers in LoRA-A. (a) The global performance. (b) The client performance.
<span id="A1.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Min K</span> means editing the layers that have the top-<span id="A1.F6.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">K</span> <span id="A1.F6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">minimum</span> similarities between global and client layer parameters. The evaluation is conducted on SAM-LLaVA dataset with 60% missing ratio.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Min 1</span> configuration consistently outperforms others in both global and personalized metrics.
We could also observe that increasing the number of edited layers beyond one does not yield performance gains.
Instead, editing more layers (e.g., <span id="A1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Min 5</span> and <span id="A1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Min 7</span>), the performance exhibits standstill or declining trends. This is more obvious for the client performance -<span id="A1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Min 3</span>, <span id="A1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Min 5</span>, and <span id="A1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Min 7</span> all show significant declines.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This is likely because each client model has personalized training parameters, and extensive editing of parameters after each fine-tuning may lead to the loss of personalized capabilities. Editing a larger number of low-similarity LoRA layers does not enhance performance and may be detrimental. Selecting only the single least similar LoRA layer (Min-1) for editing proves to be the most effective strategy for both global and personalized objectives.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Analysis for Efficiency and Resource Consumption</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Analysis for Efficiency</h3>

<figure id="A2.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>
Time consumption per epoch (seconds), including both local training and global aggregation time.
</figcaption>
<div id="A2.T4.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:34.4pt;vertical-align:-13.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(45.8pt,-4.6pt) scale(1.36169081041547,1.36169081041547) ;">
<table id="A2.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Method</th>
<td id="A2.T4.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">HetLoRA</td>
<td id="A2.T4.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">FloRA</td>
<td id="A2.T4.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">FediLoRA</td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Time per Epoch (s)</th>
<td id="A2.T4.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">434.17</td>
<td id="A2.T4.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">401.20</td>
<td id="A2.T4.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">415.33</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a comparison of time consumption between our method and baseline algorithms (Table <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ B.1 Analysis for Efficiency ‣ Appendix B Analysis for Efficiency and Resource Consumption ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Among them, FLoRA incurs the lowest overhead, as its concatenation strategy does not require additional weight allocation. In contrast, HetLoRA exhibits the highest cost due to its computation of Frobenius norms across multiple LoRA layers to redefine aggregation weights, which introduces significant overhead. Our method demonstrates moderate time consumption, comparable to FLoRA, with the main complexity arising from the dimension-wise aggregation process (as detailed in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Dimension-wise Reweighting ‣ 3 FediLoRA ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). Overall, our approach achieves better trade-offs in both time efficiency and performance compared to HetLoRA.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Analysis for Resources Storage</h3>

<figure id="A2.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>
Comparison of extra memory storage requirements per client per epoch (in MiB) among different federated learning frameworks. CreamFL and CACMRN are representative approaches for handling missing modalities.
</figcaption>
<div id="A2.T5.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:29.5pt;vertical-align:-11.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(25.1pt,-2.1pt) scale(1.1703479874602,1.1703479874602) ;">
<table id="A2.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A2.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A2.T5.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="A2.T5.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A2.T5.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FediLoRA</span></th>
<th id="A2.T5.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A2.T5.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CreamFL</span></th>
<th id="A2.T5.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="A2.T5.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CACMRN</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A2.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A2.T5.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">Extra Storage (MiB)</th>
<td id="A2.T5.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">16</td>
<td id="A2.T5.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>500</td>
<td id="A2.T5.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><math id="A2.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>2000</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate the feasibility of deploying our method within large language models (LLMs) in Table <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ B.2 Analysis for Resources Storage ‣ Appendix B Analysis for Efficiency and Resource Consumption ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. The additional storage overhead depends on the number of local LoRA-A matrices, which typically account for less than 2% of the overall LLMs size. CreamFL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2023)</a></cite> and CACMRN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiong et al. (2023)</a></cite> are traditional federated learning methods for handling missing modalities. However, CreamFL incurs communication time and memory overhead due to its need to store multiple batches of global representations for contrastive learning. CACMRN reconstructs representations by maintaining multiple generative models, resulting in significant per-client storage costs. Given these overheads, their approaches are impractical for adaptation to LLMs, while FediLoRA remains lightweight and scalable.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Analysis of Edited-Layer over Training Duration</h2>

<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="A3.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/layers2/recaps_0.4_fedilora.png" id="A3.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((a)) </span>Recaps-118K (40% missing ratio)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="A3.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/layers2/recaps_0.5_fedilora.png" id="A3.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((b)) </span>Recaps-118K (60% missing ratio)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="A3.F7.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/layers2/sam_0.4_fedilora.png" id="A3.F7.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((c)) </span>SAM-LLaVA (40% missing ratio)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="A3.F7.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/layers2/sam_0.5_fedilora.png" id="A3.F7.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((d)) </span>SAM-LLaVA (60% missing ratio)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_many">
<figure id="A3.F7.sf5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/layers2/next_0.4_fedilora.png" id="A3.F7.sf5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((e)) </span>Next-Preference (40% missing ratio)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A3.F7.sf6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.06984v1/pic/layers2/next_0.5_fedilora.png" id="A3.F7.sf6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">((f)) </span>Next-Preference (60% missing ratio)</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Visualization of the edited layer. The height of each bar corresponds to the transformer layer index of the edited LoRA module in the LLaVA model, which contains a total of 32 Transformer blocks. Taller bars indicate layers closer to the output layer. The color gradient from red to blue reflects the similarity between the edited local module and its corresponding global counterpart, with red indicating lower similarity and blue indicating higher similarity.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section aims to investigate how the selected edited layer evolves during training. We visualize the edited layers during training in three datasets and two missing raito. Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Analysis of Edited-Layer over Training Duration ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> illustrates the similarity between client-side and global LoRA parameters across epochs on three datasets. We define layers closer to the output as <span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deeper</span> layers and those near the input as <span id="A3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">shallow</span> layers. As shown in Figure <a href="#A3.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Analysis of Edited-Layer over Training Duration ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> and  <a href="#A3.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Analysis of Edited-Layer over Training Duration ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>, the initial similarity is around 0.90–0.91, indicating noticeable divergence in early fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across all datasets, a consistent pattern emerges: in the early epochs, <span id="A3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">deeper layers show lower similarity</span> and are more frequently edited. As training progresses, similarity gradually increases—rising from <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.90 to <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.96 in Recaps-118K (Figure <a href="#A3.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Analysis of Edited-Layer over Training Duration ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> and  <a href="#A3.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Analysis of Edited-Layer over Training Duration ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>), and from <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.94 to <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.98 in SAM-LLaVA (Figure <a href="#A3.F7.sf3" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Analysis of Edited-Layer over Training Duration ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(c)</span></a> and  <a href="#A3.F7.sf4" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Analysis of Edited-Layer over Training Duration ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(d)</span></a>) and Next-Preference, suggesting convergence between local and global LoRA parameters (Figure <a href="#A3.F7.sf5" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Analysis of Edited-Layer over Training Duration ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(e)</span></a> and  <a href="#A3.F7.sf6" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Analysis of Edited-Layer over Training Duration ‣ FediLoRA: Heterogeneous LoRA for Federated Multimodal Fine-tuning under Missing Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(f)</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These findings suggest that deeper LoRA layers exhibit greater divergence from the global model in the early stages of training and thus are edited more frequently.
Over time, local-global similarity increases, indicating convergence. Under severe missing modality conditions, the tendency to edit deeper layers becomes more obvious, likely due to their role in compensating for disrupted semantic representations. This aligns with prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2023b)</a></cite> that deeper layers are more adaptive when semantic information is incomplete or noisy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
